<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?><transcript><text start="0" dur="5.28">This might be the most ambitious long</text><text start="3" dur="4.8">shot precision experiment that I&amp;#39;ve ever</text><text start="5.28" dur="4.68">seen. Scientists have put one atomic</text><text start="7.8" dur="4.72">clock down in Boulder, Colorado and one</text><text start="9.96" dur="3.56">up in this observatory 4,000 m above sea</text><text start="12.52" dur="3.04">level and they&amp;#39;re going to try and</text><text start="13.52" dur="4.44">measure the difference in time dilation</text><text start="15.56" dur="3.96">according to general relativity.</text><text start="17.96" dur="3.64">Because up here, time [music] itself</text><text start="19.52" dur="4.48">should be passing faster relative to</text><text start="21.6" dur="4.16">down in Boulder.</text><text start="24" dur="3.68">&amp;gt;&amp;gt; If you go into a science center, often</text><text start="25.76" dur="3.32">they will explain gravity as the bending</text><text start="27.68" dur="3.76">of space-time and what they will have is</text><text start="29.08" dur="3.92">a little funnel here the particle goes</text><text start="31.44" dur="4.32">[music] around the funnel and that&amp;#39;s</text><text start="33" dur="4.16">meant to give you an impression of why</text><text start="35.76" dur="3.48">the Earth goes around the Sun. But</text><text start="37.16" dur="4.48">that&amp;#39;s not actually what happens. It&amp;#39;s</text><text start="39.24" dur="4.72">not as much that space is curving, it&amp;#39;s</text></transcript>